    // /dns-propagation-checker/
    dpc_h1: 'DNS propagation checker — see when your DNS changes go live',
    dpc_subtitle: 'Resolve any domain to its current A, AAAA, MX, TXT, NS, CNAME, and SOA records. Free, DNS-over-HTTPS, no login.',
    dpc_intro_h2: 'What is DNS propagation?',
    dpc_intro_text: 'DNS (Domain Name System) is the internet\'s address book — it translates human-readable names like example.com into IP addresses. When you change a DNS record, the change does not appear everywhere at once. Each DNS resolver caches the previous answer until the record\'s TTL (time to live) expires, then it asks the authoritative server again. This staggered refresh is what people call "DNS propagation".',
    dpc_what_h2: 'Why DNS changes take time to propagate',
    dpc_what_text: 'Resolvers around the world cache DNS answers to reduce load on the authoritative servers. Each cached answer comes with a TTL set by the authoritative server. While the TTL is valid, the resolver returns the cached answer without re-querying. Once it expires, the resolver asks again and gets the new record. The result: between the moment you publish a change and the moment the last resolver refreshes, different visitors around the world can see different answers.',
    dpc_how_h2: 'How this tool helps you check propagation',
    dpc_how_text: 'This tool queries Google DNS-over-HTTPS and Cloudflare DNS-over-HTTPS in parallel for every common record type and shows you the answer from each resolver. If the two resolvers agree, you have a consistent view. If they disagree, the older one is still serving a cached answer — propagation is in progress.',
    dpc_ttl_h2: 'Typical propagation time',
    dpc_ttl_text: 'Most changes propagate within minutes to a few hours. Common authoritative TTLs are 300 seconds (5 min) to 3600 seconds (1 hour). Larger TTLs (12h, 24h, 48h) used to be common for stability and can prolong propagation. Some resolvers ignore the TTL and use their own cap, which is why a change can occasionally take longer than the record\'s TTL would suggest.',
    dpc_faq_h2: 'Frequently asked questions',
    dpc_faq_1_q: 'How long does DNS propagation really take?',
    dpc_faq_1_a: 'For most records with a short TTL (5–60 minutes), propagation is fast — most resolvers refresh within an hour. For records with a long TTL (12–48 hours) or for large TTL changes, propagation can take up to 48 hours to fully complete across the global resolver fleet.',
    dpc_faq_2_q: 'Why do different DNS lookup tools show different answers?',
    dpc_faq_2_a: 'Each tool uses different upstream resolvers (Google, Cloudflare, OpenDNS, ISP resolvers, etc.) and each resolver caches answers independently. Until the TTL expires on a given resolver, it returns the cached answer. If you see different answers across tools, propagation is in progress.',
    dpc_faq_3_q: 'Can I speed up DNS propagation?',
    dpc_faq_3_a: 'Not directly — propagation is governed by the TTLs already in the wild. But you can lower the TTL on a record 24–48 hours before the change so that the next refresh happens quickly. After the change is in place, you can raise the TTL back to a longer value to reduce resolver load.',
    dpc_faq_4_q: 'What is DNS-over-HTTPS?',
    dpc_faq_4_a: 'DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) encrypts DNS queries so that no one on the path between you and the resolver can see what domains you are looking up. Traditional DNS sends queries in cleartext, which means your ISP, coffee-shop Wi-Fi, or anyone else on the network can log every site you visit. This tool uses DoH (via Google and Cloudflare) so your lookups are private.',

    // /vpn-checker/
    vpc_h1: 'VPN checker — is my VPN actually working?',
    vpc_subtitle: 'See whether your real IP is exposed, hidden behind a VPN, routed through a proxy, or going over the Tor network. Free, instant, no login.',
    vpc_intro_h2: 'How to tell if your VPN is working',
    vpc_intro_text: 'When a VPN is working, the rest of the internet should see the VPN server\'s IP, not yours. The VPN server\'s IP belongs to a hosting provider, the city shown will be the city the VPN server is in (often very different from your real city), and the connection type will be flagged as a data center or VPN. This tool reads all of those signals from your current connection and shows them in plain English.',
    vpc_how_h2: 'How VPN detection works',
    vpc_how_text: 'Every public IP belongs to an autonomous system (AS) — usually an ISP, a hosting provider, or a mobile carrier. VPN providers use hosting provider IPs because the VPN servers themselves run in data centers. This tool cross-references the IP\'s AS and registered organization against known VPN, proxy, and Tor exit-node lists, and flags the connection type accordingly.',
    vpc_signs_h2: 'Signs your VPN is not working',
    vpc_signs_text: 'A few quick signals that your VPN is leaking: the city shown is your real city, the ISP is your real ISP (not a hosting provider), and the connection type reads "residential" or "business" instead of "hosting" or "VPN". If the tool flags your connection as exposed while you have a VPN client running, your VPN tunnel is down and traffic is going over your regular connection.',
    vpc_faq_h2: 'Frequently asked questions',
    vpc_faq_1_q: 'How do I know my VPN is connected?',
    vpc_faq_1_a: 'Open this page while your VPN is running. If the tool shows a different country and city from your real one, and the connection type is "hosting" or "VPN", your tunnel is up. If it shows your real city and your real ISP, the tunnel is down — every site you visit is seeing your real IP.',
    vpc_faq_2_q: 'Can a VPN hide my IP completely?',
    vpc_faq_2_a: 'From the sites you visit, yes. From your VPN provider, no — the provider sees your real IP because your traffic enters their network before exiting to the public internet. That is why choosing a reputable VPN provider with a no-logs policy matters. From public IP geolocation databases, the IP you project is the VPN server\'s IP, which is what this tool shows.',
    vpc_faq_3_q: 'Does Tor show up as a VPN?',
    vpc_faq_3_a: 'No. Tor exit nodes are tracked separately. This tool flags Tor connections specifically so you can tell the three apart at a glance: regular connection, VPN, proxy, or Tor.',
    vpc_faq_4_q: 'My VPN shows my real city — what is wrong?',
    vpc_faq_4_a: 'A few common causes: the VPN tunnel dropped mid-session (most VPN clients reconnect automatically but the gap can leak traffic), the VPN is configured to use split tunneling for browser traffic (only some apps go through the tunnel), or the VPN client hasn\'t finished handshaking before you loaded the page. Disconnect, reconnect, and re-check.',

